"""E57 job 1280 (recompute oracle): LIGHT pilot-informed registration text, v12 (server repo copy; DRAFT; text only, no new .md file). Analysis code: e57_1280_analyze.py. External record = a SIGNED tag pushed to a private remote repository after v12 is complete and re-checked and before the first confirmatory seed is run; the checker (check_external_record) compares the tag signature, the tagged scripts and the REMOTE (server-side) time with the oldest result file. DELETED from earlier versions: the A / B / A-prime hash freezes, the public-commit timing evidence, the predictive-probability futility rule, the interim look and its boundaries, the blind failure-classification procedure, the SESOI derivation. Nothing in this text was written after opening any 1280, instrumentation or auxiliary-seed output (none exists)."""
import re
PLACEHOLDERS=['instrumentation k (from seeds 91..94; needs Hans GPU sentence)','private registry repository name (REGISTRY_REPO) and the push of the signed tag (Hans confirms; the origin remote of this working tree is PUBLIC)']
REGISTRATION_V12=r"""1 STATUS AND TERMS. This is a PILOT-INFORMED registered analysis: the thresholds were fixed after the 3-seed pilot (job 1279), see item 13. Unqualified pre-registration wording is not used; the term is 'pilot-informed registered analysis'. A result that is not detected is NOT evidence that there is no leak. External record: a signed tag on a commit that contains this text and e57_1280_analyze.py is pushed to a private remote repository AFTER v12 is complete and re-checked and BEFORE the first confirmatory seed is run. The record consists of TWO things stated together: (i) the commit SHA of the tagged commit and (ii) the SHA-256 of each plan file (this text, e57_1280_registration.py, and the analysis script e57_1280_analyze.py), listed in the tag message as 'sha256 <hex> <path>'; the checker verifies the signature, that each listed SHA-256 equals the SHA-256 of the file in the tagged commit and of the working-tree file, that the tagged scripts equal the working-tree scripts, and that the remote (server-side) time of the tag is older than the oldest confirmatory result file. Reviewer access: the registry repository is private; the commit SHA and the plan-file SHA-256 values are stated in the submission, and at submission the tagged files are made available either publicly or as an anonymized archive (anonymous.4open.science or an anonymous repository export), so a reviewer can recompute the hashes and see that the plan predates the results. ORDER-EVIDENCE PROCEDURE: FREEZE A = the signed tag contains ONLY the plan files and code (this text, e57_1280_registration.py, e57_1280_analyze.py); no result, no scheduler log and no results listing is put into the tag. (1) At the push, record the tag name, the tag-object SHA, the commit SHA and the release created_at. (2) Later, in a separate VERIFICATION RECORD created after the confirmatory run, record the listing of the results directory with full-precision modification times, the SHA-256 of the first confirmatory output file and of the scheduler log (queue.log, whose job-start line carries the start time) as it was when that file appeared; this record is then compared with the release created_at of step (1) (the release time must be older than the first output file and than the job-start line). Both records are kept in the submission materials. WHAT THIS CHECK DOES NOT PROVE: it does not prove that the tag was never deleted and re-created, because a tag in a repository controlled by the author can be removed and pushed again (a re-created tag has a new tag-object SHA and release time, which exposes a change only if the earlier values were recorded elsewhere, as in step 2); the release created_at of a private repository is GitHub's claim, which a reviewer cannot verify independently; file modification times and the scheduler log are on a machine controlled by the author and can be altered; it does not prove that nobody saw any 1280 or related result before the tag (only the disclosure in item 13 covers that); and it does not prove that the plan was followed after the tag (only the analysis script run on the stored outputs does). It shows that files with these hashes were recorded by the remote before the first confirmatory output file existed, nothing stronger.
2 DESIGN. n = 30 confirmatory seeds (101..130), one fixed-sample analysis; no interim look, no stopping, no extension; unit = seed. Instrumentation seeds 91..94 (variance components at k = 8 only) and the auxiliary NATS 16->128 seeds 901..908 (descriptive only) are separate from the confirmatory seeds and never enter them. Every arm and every seed result is reported.
3 CLAIMS AND GATES (table CLAIMS_TABLE; one-sided, alpha = .05 each; intersection-union hierarchy 3a -> 3b -> 3c, size <= .05, NOT multiplicity-corrected). E-form: E = NLL(blocked) - NLL(not blocked); E_cache = a - nc, E_rec = b - c, E_mask = recomputed band NLL - recomputed natural NLL (nat/token); margins G = M_NATMASK - M_NATS, H = M_RECOMP - M_NATS (nat/fact). Contrasts T_R = E_cache - .8 E_rec, T_Q = E_rec - .7 E_mask, L_F = H - .7 G; placebo: PLd = M_RECOMP_PL - M_NATS with one-sided upper confidence bound < delta_PL = .25 nat (absolute). Gates 2a (E_rec > 0, mean >= .25), 2b (E_mask > 0, mean >= .10), 2c (G > 0). Verdict 4 CACHE SUFFICIENT = gate 2a and T_R > 0, a separate claim at its own .05. Flags (not claims): F_LOW, H_GEN, HALF_PL, ANOMALY, G_PRACTICAL.
4 TESTS AND WORDING. Each test is run twice: the seed-paired t test (df = 29) and the exact sign-flip test. 'Significant' is written only when BOTH reject. When the t test rejects and the exact test does not, the wording is 'supported, distribution-sensitive'. When only the exact test rejects there is no claim (flag SIGNFLIP_ONLY). A step that does not reject is written 'not detected', which is not evidence of absence. HOLM: the intersection-union claim is not corrected; in addition the four component tests (T_R leak, T_Q, L_F, placebo) are Holm-adjusted over four tests at level .05, using for each test the LARGER of its two p-values, max(p_t, p_exact); a component without a Holm-adjusted rejection that is reported outside the intersection claim is labelled EXPLORATORY. The wording of every outcome is drafted in OUTCOME_PHRASES before the results exist.
5 SIGN-FLIP: EXACT COMPUTATION AND LIMITS. The exact one-sided p = P(S >= S_obs), S = sum of s_i x_i with s_i = +-1 uniform, is computed over ALL 2^30 sign vectors by MEET IN THE MIDDLE, not by enumerating 2^30 vectors: the left 15 signs give 2^15 partial sums a, the right 15 signs give 2^15 partial sums b, b is sorted once, and for each a the number of b with a + b >= S_obs is counted by binary search (cost about 2^15 x 15 operations); the count is an exact integer and p = count / 2^30. INTEGER ARITHMETIC RULE: every seed-level value is rounded to an integer multiple of 1e-9 (nat) before the test and all sums are exact int64 (overflow guarded), so there is no floating-point tolerance and ties are decided exactly; the denominator is the exact 2^30. The OBSERVED statistic sum(x_i) is computed on the same 1e-9 integer scale as the null distribution, and the guard max|d| x n x 1e9 < 2^63 is asserted; 'exact' therefore holds for the ROUNDED contrasts (multiples of 1e-9 nat), not for the unrounded floating-point values, and the rounding can change a p value only for contrasts that differ from a tie by less than 1e-9. VERIFICATION (selftest): (a) signflip_count_exact equals a brute-force enumeration of all 2^n sign vectors with Python integers for n = 1..16 and n = 20 (random, integer-valued, tied, zero and constant data), counts identical; (b) DIRECT checks at n = 27 and n = 30 against an independent exact algorithm (dynamic programming over the distribution of the signed sums, integer inputs with zeros and repeated values), counts identical; (c) an independent Monte Carlo of 1e6 sign vectors on an unstructured n = 30 input, tolerance fixed in advance: |p_MC - p_exact| <= 5 sqrt(p (1 - p) / 1e6) + 1e-6; (d) closed forms: all contrasts zero gives p = 1, all equal positive gives p = 2^-n, equal magnitudes with k positive and n - k negative give the binomial tail P(Bin(n, 1/2) >= k), p >= 2^-n always, the comparison is >= (ties included) and the denominator is 2^n, and random inputs mixing ties and zeros, for n = 1, 2, 7, 16, 27, 30. CPU benchmark (benchmark_exact, n = 30, 48-core server, e57_1280_exact_bench_v12.json, int64 version): 0.0031 s per test, 0.025 s per analysis of 8 exact tests, 7.9 MB for the two cached int64 sign matrices; the earlier 'peak RSS 102 MB' is the whole Python process (interpreter, numpy and scipy are already about 102 MB resident before the first test, rss_at_start_MB in the file) and not the memory of the algorithm, whose own arrays are the 7.9 MB sign matrices plus a few 2^15-entry temporaries; so the exact computation is feasible and the Monte Carlo fallback is not used; the exact routine refuses n > 32. If this were infeasible the pre-specified fallback is a Monte Carlo sign-flip with >= 1e6 sign vectors, numpy PCG64 seed 20261112, p = (1 + count) / (1 + 1e6). LIMITS: (a) the test is exact only under symmetry of the seed contrast about 0 under H0; it tests symmetry about zero, not mean zero, so under a skewed contrast it can reject for a reason other than a nonzero mean; in a simulation of skewed contrasts (shifted lognormal, sigma 1.5, mean shift .35, n = 30, 3000 datasets) the t test alone rejected in 1 and the exact test alone in 9, which is why an exact-only rejection is never a claim; (b) the smallest attainable p is 2^-30, so resolution is not a constraint at n = 30; (c) ties are counted as at least as extreme (>=, so a sign vector whose sum equals the observed sum counts toward p; conservative), and exact zeros contribute zero to every sum; (d) it is a sensitivity partner of the t test, not a distribution-free replacement of the estimand.
6 SEEDS AND FAILURES. A failure is a seed whose run diverges or yields a non-finite value. At most 3 failed seeds are replaced, in order, by 131, 132, 133; every failure is reported with its seed and kind; beyond the cap the analysis uses the remaining seeds and reports n. A failure is never classified by its outcome (fill_seeds raises on any other reason).
7 LOWER BOUND ON n AND MISSING SEEDS. n_min = 27 (Hans 2026-11-11). With fewer than 27 seeds the whole result is labelled EXPLORATORY. Whenever seeds are missing (n < 30) a ZERO-EFFECT sensitivity analysis is reported (it replaces the earlier name 'worst-case sensitivity', because zero effect is the null value, not the most unfavourable value in every direction): every missing seed is imputed as ZERO EFFECT (all contrasts and gate quantities 0) and the analysis is repeated with n = 30; the verdict is called robust to missing seeds only if its level is unchanged (final_report).
8 SESOI. SESOI_fact = .5 nat/fact, FIXED by decision (Hans 2026-11-11), irrespective of the pilot uncertainty; there is no coefficient, no derivation from the auxiliary seeds and no replacement rule; nat/token has no SESOI. SESOI enters no test. G_PRACTICAL = TRUE only when the one-sided 95% lower bound of G is >= .5 nat/fact. delta_PL = .25 nat absolute.
9 CONFOUND TABLE (CONFOUND_TABLE): placebo and oracle arms are matched in masked length (exactly) and position (mean masked position within 64 tokens) and checked for attention mass; attention mass is NOT matched and is reported as a covariate per seed (confound_check); a length or position mismatch is reported with the seed. Rationale for 64 tokens: it is one quarter of the 256-token band, so the oracle and its placebo spans stay in the same band quarter and their distance to the read position differs by at most a quarter of the band; this is a design choice, not a measured equivalence margin.
10 GENERALITY BRIDGE (one candidate, BRIDGE_CANDIDATE): public-QA write stream NATS vs NATMASK, exploratory, not part of the 3c claim.
11 POWER AND GPU TIME AT n = 30 AND n = 27: POWER_NOTE (planning basis SD .225 = pilot upper limit, weak evidence because df = 2; SD .45 is an ASSUMED SCENARIO and .90 an assumed stress scenario; component power, 3c power and overall IUT + Holm power are separate; numbers in e57_1280_power_v12_n30.json and e57_1280_power_v12_n27.json), GPU hours in GPU_NOTE; values >= .9999 are printed '>=.9999'; no claim of achieving a power target is made.
12 1284 IS A SEPARATE FAMILY: seeds 201..220, its own alpha = .05 one-sided, primary RECOMP-ORACLE - RECOMP-PL, key secondary HALF-PL - HALF; it is not pooled with 1280 and shares no seed (checked).
13 DISCLOSURE. Pilot (job 1279, seeds 62..64, K0 = 0, exploratory, 2 degrees of freedom, excluded from the confirmatory set): X_ORACLE .076 / .065 / .079; X_MASK .337 / .367 / .383; PLACEBO - NATS +.096 / +.036 / -.003 (mean +.043, SD .050); RAND - NATS +.048 / -.010 / -.022. Thresholds fixed after the pilot: .25, .10, .8, .7, .7, .3, SESOI .5, 1.2, 3 of 32 unmatched, delta_PL .25, n = 30, n_min = 27, the cap of 3 replacements, the 64-token position tolerance. Expectation (not a prediction): E_cache is small relative to E_rec (pilot .07 vs .36), so if gate 2a passes the leak direction follows almost automatically; the T_R leak result is therefore not counted as independent confirmation. Power statements are scenario estimates.
14 DELETED FROM EARLIER VERSIONS: A / B / A-prime hash freezes, public-commit timing evidence (replaced by the signed-tag external record), predictive-probability futility, interim look and boundaries, blind failure classification, informative-failure sensitivity, SESOI derivation and its fallback. Archived in git (commits up to 09793bf408e7a3afd165a487050aa3c698d015c9)."""
